{"title":"1999 AMC 8 Problems/Problem 25","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Competition_Solution_(speed)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Competition Solution (speed)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_3\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Solution_4\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 4</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Solution_5\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 5</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Solution_6\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 6</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#Solution_7\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">9</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 7</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-10\"><a href=\"#Solution_8\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">10</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 8</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-11\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_1_by_CosineMethod\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">11</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution 1 by CosineMethod</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-12\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">12</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_25&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/f/9ffb448918db29f2a72f8f87f421b3b3cad18f95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/b/b/abb5588023cfa1ac14643e9778699f03eecc57a3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$J$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> are midpoints of the sides of right triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/a/f/8af68a1f04db9f1548784f5b53a43d0f8964acbb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ACG$\" width=\"42\" height=\"13\" />. Points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/f/b/dfb064112b6c94470339f6571f69d07afc1c024c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$K$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"19\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/2/fa2fa899f0afb05d6837885523503a2d4df434f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/7/027f4a11d6090f9eac0ce2488df6384dad1263ea.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$I$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> are midpoints of the sides of triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/1/6/21680e06ba4402b3050b1dd75c523f06750d8296.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$JDG$\" width=\"41\" height=\"12\" />, etc. If the dividing and shading process is done 100 times (the first three are shown) and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/d/0/4d072247f9ad6d38b00cd65a457cd117d2a2a612.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AC=CG=6$\" width=\"113\" height=\"13\" />, then the total area of the shaded triangles is nearest\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/f/d/cfd00c85535b42635e07b9230f8dc34f5d21d985.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] draw((0,0)--(6,0)--(6,6)--cycle); draw((3,0)--(3,3)--(6,3)); draw((4.5,3)--(4.5,4.5)--(6,4.5)); draw((5.25,4.5)--(5.25,5.25)--(6,5.25)); fill((3,0)--(6,0)--(6,3)--cycle,black); fill((4.5,3)--(6,3)--(6,4.5)--cycle,black); fill((5.25,4.5)--(6,4.5)--(6,5.25)--cycle,black);  label(&quot;$A$&quot;,(0,0),SW); label(&quot;$B$&quot;,(3,0),S); label(&quot;$C$&quot;,(6,0),SE); label(&quot;$D$&quot;,(6,3),E); label(&quot;$E$&quot;,(6,4.5),E); label(&quot;$F$&quot;,(6,5.25),E); label(&quot;$G$&quot;,(6,6),NE); label(&quot;$H$&quot;,(5.25,5.25),NW); label(&quot;$I$&quot;,(4.5,4.5),NW); label(&quot;$J$&quot;,(3,3),NW); label(&quot;$K$&quot;,(4.5,3),S); label(&quot;$L$&quot;,(5.25,4.5),S); [/asy]\" width=\"252\" height=\"248\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/2/2/d2217a7b555c3e7185419a06ceaf7e56179789d5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{(A)}\\ 6 \\qquad \\text{(B)}\\ 7 \\qquad \\text{(C)}\\ 8 \\qquad \\text{(D)}\\ 9 \\qquad \\text{(E)}\\ 10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"339\" height=\"17\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Competition_Solution_.28speed.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Competition_Solution_(speed)\">Competition Solution (speed)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_25&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Competition Solution (speed)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>If you want speed, this is your solution.\n</p><p>It is easy to see each of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/2/5/a258ab8ab08148f0cf470182b67e54a94c244fb6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BCDJ, \\dots$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"89\" height=\"18\" /> are squares, and thus the joined triangles are congruent. In addition, by HL, the external most triangle is also congruent to the two.\n</p><p>Let the process repeat indefinitely from the principle of geometric series, and notice that the area degenerates to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/7/7/277808a033b0d645b6df93e4ec60050444c8ad88.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"25\" height=\"14\" />, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> is the area of the shaded region. The area itself if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/9/7c9b1cb07c4f358d3a0d839bd3cbee6f3606c27f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 36 = 18$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"89\" height=\"38\" />, hence <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/8/818297b710359102c7cd36d1ce433f64657cc77d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A = 6$\" width=\"46\" height=\"13\" />. Because the 100th iteration gets closer and closer to this value, it follows the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/b/adb760a2c7143780db481b394ada34e0bb23b3bc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{6}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"22\" height=\"25\" />.\n</p><p>Many other solutions have the same idea as this one, but they do not formally introduce this concept rather they jump to it, without any logic.\n</p><p>~Pinotation\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_25&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/7/2/f722737ea859dab2d8c9e3d24325ecac6c28ac9b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle FGH$\" width=\"61\" height=\"13\" /> is fairly small relative to the rest of the diagram, we can make an underestimate by using the current diagram.  All triangles are right-isosceles triangles.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/b/1/7b10d23edc5baf3f4c21989151bb077d37dc45e1.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[CD = \\frac {CG}{2} = 3, DE = \\frac{CD}{2} = \\frac{3}{2}, EF = \\frac{DE}{2} = \\frac{3}{4}\\]\" width=\"384\" height=\"37\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/0/f20678c40b88333a209fd9980d0f7b1e0ff91e68.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[CB = CD = 3, DK = DE = \\frac{3}{2}, EL = EF = \\frac{3}{4}\\]\" width=\"371\" height=\"37\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/0/2/d02cfac86d2e832f8ba971b4d590414b11e7e27d.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[[CBD] = \\frac{1}{2}3^2 = \\frac{9}{2}\\]\" width=\"142\" height=\"37\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/3/8/d3878effd6f14287a2ffb50b9ce85d25ade53944.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[[DKE] = \\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{3}{2})^2 = \\frac{9}{8}\\]\" width=\"162\" height=\"37\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/a/adadcd328080a63e06f87d1a28a5dbb0d2eb767c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[[ELF] = \\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{3}{4})^2 = \\frac{9}{32}\\]\" width=\"166\" height=\"37\" />\n</p><p>The sum of the shaded regions is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/a/57ac466832c36369f0fcc483d15703ce861a64a0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{9}{2} + \\frac{9}{8} + \\frac{9}{32} = \\frac{189}{32} \\approx 5.9$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"195\" height=\"37\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/0/c/d0c440106c5ffa00bea92f12995243fe314535ec.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5.9$\" width=\"22\" height=\"12\" /> is an underestimate, as some portion (but not all) of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/7/2/f722737ea859dab2d8c9e3d24325ecac6c28ac9b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle FGH$\" width=\"61\" height=\"13\" /> will be shaded in future iterations.  \n</p><p>If you shade all of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/7/2/f722737ea859dab2d8c9e3d24325ecac6c28ac9b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle FGH$\" width=\"61\" height=\"13\" />, this will add an additional <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/8/0/680911add1c8b07d43c83b36a1ece04206f49253.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{9}{32}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"20\" height=\"37\" /> to the area, giving <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/c/17ca9e0a367a01d5f447ab116977511057260b5c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{198}{32} \\approx 6.2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"78\" height=\"37\" />, which is an overestimate.\n</p><p>Thus, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/d/3/1d3c3da216f40540d75df5473d2fc7cda174ed78.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\text{(A)}\\ 6}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"53\" height=\"30\" /> is the only answer that is both over the underestimate and under the overestimate.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_25&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>In iteration <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, congruent triangles <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/1/8/f180fcddc7fc9486d583d1dae68ec4e5abfe75d0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABJ,  \\triangle BDJ,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"122\" height=\"17\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/a/7/1a7a0327109ede54bf3abf9eb325bb58a503afc2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle BDC$\" width=\"60\" height=\"13\" /> are created, with one of them being shaded.\n</p><p>In iteration <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, three more congruent triangles are created, with one of them being shaded.\n</p><p>As the process continues indefinitely, in each row, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/e/1/4e1965f8d70832ded66434c75a7bd52b75090af0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" /> of each triplet of new congruent triangles will be shaded.  The \"fourth triangle\" at the top (<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/7/2/f722737ea859dab2d8c9e3d24325ecac6c28ac9b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle FGH$\" width=\"61\" height=\"13\" /> in the diagram) will gradually shrink, \n</p><p>leaving about <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/e/1/4e1965f8d70832ded66434c75a7bd52b75090af0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" /> of the area shaded.  This means <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/6/9/6697382d887cbd74224ec8dd252d96571d04a959.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{3}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}6\\cdot 6\\right) = 6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"119\" height=\"43\" /> square units will be shaded when the process goes on indefinitely, giving <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/6/e/f6eb65962292d994ce2f9a8af63728883f256b18.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{A}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"27\" height=\"25\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3\">Solution 3</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_25&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Using Solution 1 as a template, note that the sum of the areas forms a <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Geometric_series\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Geometric series\">geometric series</a>:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/a/1/ea18d230bd0e0f3f5cdaccf47afc23450c69aee8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{9}{2} + \\frac{9}{8} + \\frac{9}{32} + \\frac{9}{128} + ...$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"182\" height=\"38\" />\n</p><p>This is the sum of a geometric series with first term <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/8/a/c8a42bccd613938fdd35e76726f54ed5e6d987b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_1 = \\frac{9}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"53\" height=\"37\" /> and common ratio <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/b/2/1b204b4563ae20284c75ab32143e6eae1af294cd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r = \\frac{1}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"44\" height=\"37\" /> This is the easiest way to do this problem.\n</p><p>The sum of an infinite geometric series with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/1/8/318e44029533122ca0079bcdcc32e5fadbbaf38b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|r|&lt;1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"51\" height=\"18\" /> is shown by the formula. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/5/c/95c2e341d9190bcb306ea2a815c37ca6c6121e8b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S_{\\infty} = \\frac{a_1}{1 - r}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"92\" height=\"33\" /> Insert the values to get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/f/7/6f73a2587a6d02bf4661a0859020edccfa239b8a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{\\frac{9}{2}}{1 - \\frac{1}{4}} = \\frac{9}{2}\\cdot\\frac{4}{3} = 6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -19px\" width=\"142\" height=\"47\" />, giving an answer of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/6/e/f6eb65962292d994ce2f9a8af63728883f256b18.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{A}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"27\" height=\"25\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_4\">Solution 4</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_25&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 4\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Find the area of the bottom triangle, which is 4.5. Notice that the area of the triangles is divided by 4 every time. 4.5*5/4≈5.7, and 5.7+(1/4)≈5.9. We can clearly see that the sum is approaching answer choice <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/6/e/f6eb65962292d994ce2f9a8af63728883f256b18.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{A}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"27\" height=\"25\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_5\">Solution 5</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_25&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 5\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The area of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/0/c/00cb04571fd7efc09d2de1b2da589ee6a5b7756d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$DBC$\" width=\"44\" height=\"12\" /> is clearly <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/e/1/4e1965f8d70832ded66434c75a7bd52b75090af0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" /> of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/4/fa4cba36332922f6449e448adaeb86065f686e29.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AJDC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"57\" height=\"14\" />, the area of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/7/2/a720add6aac2f048887df745a31d63096cbd2f0c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$DEK$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"49\" height=\"14\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/e/1/4e1965f8d70832ded66434c75a7bd52b75090af0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" /> of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/6/f/a6f5be6dbde753789bf9e69eeb2e8655d6039c20.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$JIED$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"53\" height=\"14\" />, if the progress is going to infinity, the shaded triangles will be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/e/1/4e1965f8d70832ded66434c75a7bd52b75090af0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" /> of the triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/a/f/8af68a1f04db9f1548784f5b53a43d0f8964acbb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ACG$\" width=\"42\" height=\"13\" />. However, 100 times is much enough. The answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/4/0/d40ac91cd3c937f01f0be9676507625ea8945e1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{3}\\times 6\\times 6\\times \\frac{1}{2} = \\boxed{(A)6}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"185\" height=\"38\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_6\">Solution 6</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_25&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 6\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Because all of the triangles are isosceles, this gives us the area of one triangle with side length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/7/f37bba504894945c07a32f5496d74299a37aa51c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/e/0/3e09bcc5d2d37bec155c8cc74d21cddb86da376b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A = \\frac{1}{2}s^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"69\" height=\"38\" />. Logically, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/7/f37bba504894945c07a32f5496d74299a37aa51c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" /> keeps on getting <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/9/2f960094315d60883495f9b74148e17487ee9584.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" /> as small as the previous one (<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/6/9e6de34e0dc3620b6f691de530359a6a81b57e34.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3, \\frac{3}{2}, \\frac{3}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"53\" height=\"38\" />, etc) Meaning for triangle number <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/2/f/82f8f444168d386df75351c93bc8ea1176b018c4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n, s = 3(\\frac{1}{2})^n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"99\" height=\"38\" />. Putting this into our original equation, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/d/9fd3b31384203bf1f03d0092881586522eee6706.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A = \\frac{1}{2}(3(\\frac{1}{2})^n)^2 = \\frac{9}{2}(\\frac{1}{2})^{2n} = 9(\\frac{1}{2})^{2n+1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"293\" height=\"38\" /> This means the total area is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/4/b/c4b3c595adf4fb498f3c8a5aa679a645caeca1ef.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$9\\sum^{\\infty}_{n=1} \\frac{1}{2}^{2n+1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"88\" height=\"53\" />, using exponent laws, we can simplify this to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/a/4/3a42c5eae055bfc1a79d08c3904da9c6e9c0811f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{9}{2} \\sum^{\\infty}_{n=1} \\frac{1}{4}^n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"69\" height=\"53\" />.\n</p><p>The formula for a geometric series like this is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/a/8ca4f005ee43668006ca959cb26c125837188754.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sum^{\\infty}_{n=1} a*r^n = \\frac{a}{1-r}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"143\" height=\"53\" />. Using this, we get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/9/7/497b1bf6ab99e30cb2a5e94b3df64a0d3f830f94.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{\\frac{9}{2}}{\\frac{4}{4}-\\frac{1}{4}} = \\frac{9}{2}\\times\\frac{4}{3} = 3\\times2 = \\boxed{(A)6}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -20px\" width=\"259\" height=\"49\" />.\n</p><p>~RandomMathGuy500\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_7\">Solution 7</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_25&amp;action=edit&amp;section=9\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 7\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Notice how you can fit <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/0/6/f06146a2190440b3c68ca6b1762a9d662bd5566b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle KDE$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"65\" height=\"18\" /> into the top right corner of square <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/f/8/1f808229af14a8d938aad454962165645abce146.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BCDJ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"58\" height=\"14\" />? Repeat this process by putting smaller triangles into the corner of the square and eventually, you should see that this converges to just <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c339afa03e060d959e4942be7522c0c992f4c41c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" /> of the square. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/2/f/12f372757d55c2d07adc720932c6d6e1396aa389.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle BCD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"63\" height=\"18\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/9/2f960094315d60883495f9b74148e17487ee9584.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" /> of the square and rest of the triangles make <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c339afa03e060d959e4942be7522c0c992f4c41c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" /> which totals to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/1/1/e1150a17bf1b733cb666be84d920c71fc96bb3f7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{3}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"11\" height=\"38\" /> of the square. Square <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/f/8/1f808229af14a8d938aad454962165645abce146.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BCDJ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"58\" height=\"14\" /> has side length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> so it's area is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/f/2/bf2c9074b396e3af0dea52d792660eea1c77f10f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$9$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/1/1/e1150a17bf1b733cb666be84d920c71fc96bb3f7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{3}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"11\" height=\"38\" /> of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/f/2/bf2c9074b396e3af0dea52d792660eea1c77f10f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$9$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/7/0/a7092fa22bfc58692c6ccd13c1a49d883dab6c4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{(A)6}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"50\" height=\"32\" />.\n</p><p>~RandomMathGuy500 \n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_8\">Solution 8</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_25&amp;action=edit&amp;section=10\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 8\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>As in Solution 3, the area form the geometric series <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/a/1/ea18d230bd0e0f3f5cdaccf47afc23450c69aee8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{9}{2} + \\frac{9}{8} + \\frac{9}{32} + \\frac{9}{128} + ...$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"182\" height=\"38\" />. If this is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />, than 4<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> is 18 + <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/a/1/ea18d230bd0e0f3f5cdaccf47afc23450c69aee8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{9}{2} + \\frac{9}{8} + \\frac{9}{32} + \\frac{9}{128} + ...$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"182\" height=\"38\" />.Than we take away <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> to get 3<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> = 18. When we solve for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> we get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> = 6, so the correct answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/6/e/f6eb65962292d994ce2f9a8af63728883f256b18.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{A}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"27\" height=\"25\" />.\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_1_by_CosineMethod\">Video Solution 1 by CosineMethod</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_25&amp;action=edit&amp;section=11\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution 1 by CosineMethod\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sZabsoMIf2I\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sZabsoMIf2I</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_25&amp;action=edit&amp;section=12\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_AMC_8\" title=\"1999 AMC 8\">1999 AMC 8</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_AMC_8_Problems\" title=\"1999 AMC 8 Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_AMC_8_Answer_Key\" title=\"1999 AMC 8 Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/resources.php?c=182&amp;cid=42&amp;year=1999\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_24\" title=\"1999 AMC 8 Problems/Problem 24\">Problem 24</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b>Last Question</b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"1999 AMC 8 Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"1999 AMC 8 Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"1999 AMC 8 Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"1999 AMC 8 Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"1999 AMC 8 Problems/Problem 5\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_6\" title=\"1999 AMC 8 Problems/Problem 6\">6</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_7\" title=\"1999 AMC 8 Problems/Problem 7\">7</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_8\" title=\"1999 AMC 8 Problems/Problem 8\">8</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_9\" title=\"1999 AMC 8 Problems/Problem 9\">9</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_10\" title=\"1999 AMC 8 Problems/Problem 10\">10</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_11\" title=\"1999 AMC 8 Problems/Problem 11\">11</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_12\" title=\"1999 AMC 8 Problems/Problem 12\">12</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_13\" title=\"1999 AMC 8 Problems/Problem 13\">13</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_14\" title=\"1999 AMC 8 Problems/Problem 14\">14</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_15\" title=\"1999 AMC 8 Problems/Problem 15\">15</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_16\" title=\"1999 AMC 8 Problems/Problem 16\">16</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_17\" title=\"1999 AMC 8 Problems/Problem 17\">17</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_18\" title=\"1999 AMC 8 Problems/Problem 18\">18</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_19\" title=\"1999 AMC 8 Problems/Problem 19\">19</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_20\" title=\"1999 AMC 8 Problems/Problem 20\">20</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_21\" title=\"1999 AMC 8 Problems/Problem 21\">21</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_22\" title=\"1999 AMC 8 Problems/Problem 22\">22</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_23\" title=\"1999 AMC 8 Problems/Problem 23\">23</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_24\" title=\"1999 AMC 8 Problems/Problem 24\">24</a> <b>•</b> <a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">25</a>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=AMC_8_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"AMC 8 Problems and Solutions\"> All AJHSME/AMC 8 Problems and Solutions</a></b>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<div class=\"print\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;width:100%;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center\">\n<div>\n<p><span class=\"mw-default-size\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:AMC_Logo.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/d/d1/AMC_Logo.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"97\" height=\"84\" class=\"mw-file-element\" /></a></span>\n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex-grow:1;text-align:right\">\n<p>These problems are copyrighted &#169; by the <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http